    "Invasive evaluation of patients with heart failure (HF) can provide critical diagnostic information to guide treatment decisions. Routine right heart catheterization alone is insufficient for treatment decisions. Hemodynamic evaluation might help in acute scenarios involving respiratory distress, systemic hypoperfusion, or cardiogenic shock. Endomyocardial biopsy might be beneficial in cases of suspected amyloidosis or myocarditis as it can influence treatment decisions. Coronary angiography is useful for patients suitable for revascularization. Routine invasive hemodynamic monitoring is not recommended unless the patient's condition remains uncertain or persistent symptoms occur despite treatment. The biopsy should be avoided during routine evaluations due to complication risks.": [],
